This text is the famous mythological opening to the Sumerian epic **"Enki and Ninmaḫ"** (also known as *The Creation of Mankind*), which dates back to the early 2nd millennium BCE. 

It describes a time before humans existed, when the lesser gods had to endure backbreaking labor to maintain the earth, leading them to weep and complain while the water god Enki slept obliviously in the deep abyss.

Here is the English translation of the text:

**1.** In those days, in the days when heaven and earth were created;
**2.** In those nights, in the nights when heaven and earth were created;
**3.** In those years, in the years when the fates were determined;
**4.** When the Anuna gods were born;
**5.** When the goddesses were taken in marriage;
**6.** When the goddesses were distributed in heaven and earth;
**7.** When the goddesses [...] became pregnant and gave birth;
**8.** When the gods were obliged for their food [...], bound to their dining halls;
**9.** The senior gods supervised the work, while the minor gods were carrying the heavy toil (baskets);
**10.** The gods were digging the canals and piling up the dirt in Ḫarali;
**11.** The gods, dredging the clay, began complaining about this life.
**12.** At that time, the one of great wisdom, the creator of the senior gods,
**13.** Enki—in the deep *Engur*, the subterranean waters, a place into whose depths no other god can look—
**14.** Was lying in his bed, not waking up from his sleep.
**15.** The gods were weeping and shedding tears, making lamentations.
**16.** To the one lying asleep, who was not waking up from his bed,
**17.** Namma, the primeval mother, who gave birth to the senior gods,
**18.** Brought the tears of the gods to her son:
**19.** "[...] are you really lying there, are you really sleeping?
**20.** "[...] will you not wake up?
**21.** "The gods, your own creations, are smashing their [...]!"

### Contextual Notes:
*   **Anuna (Line 4):** The pantheon of Sumerian gods (later known in Akkadian as the Anunnaki).
*   **Ḫarali (Line 10):** A mythical or distant location in Sumerian literature, often associated with mountains, wealth, or the underworld. 
*   **Engur (Line 13):** Also known as the *Abzu*, this is the cosmic subterranean ocean of fresh water where the god Enki resides.
*   **Namma (Line 17):** The primeval mother goddess of the sea who acts as the mediator between the exhausted working gods and her sleeping son, Enki. Under her prompting, Enki will eventually wake up and create humanity to take over the labor of the gods.
*   **Bracketed elements `[...]` and `X`:** These represent areas where the original clay tablet is broken, damaged, or the cuneiform signs are illegible.

